#:blog{:title
       "A Tale of Six Engines: Unreal, DOOM, Unity, Godot, Bevy, and the Dao Engine",
       :date #inst "2026-10-20T00:00:00.000-00:00",
       :abstract
       [:p
        "Game engines have spent thirty years trading authoring ergonomics against silicon performance across Unreal, DOOM, Unity, Godot, and Bevy. "
        "The Dao Engine blueprint re-envisions interactive simulation from first principles using datom.world axioms, uniting declarative authoring with flat hardware execution."],
       :content
       [:section.blog-article
        [:div.section-inner
         [:article
          :$blog-title
          :$blog-date
          [:h2 "Re-envisioning the Game Engine: The Dao Engine"]
          [:p
           [:strong "The Dao Engine does not exist yet."]
           " "
           "It is not a commercial product or a downloadable runtime today. "
           "Rather, it is an architectural re-envisioning: a blueprint for how a next-generation game engine can be built from first principles "
           "using the axioms and technologies of datom.world."]
          [:p
           "Crucially, "
           [:strong
            "the Dao Engine's design directly mirrors the Yin.vm and Yang duality of datom.world"]
           ". "
           "In datom.world, "
           [:strong "Yang"]
           " acts as the expressive, human-facing compilation frontend that translates diverse surface syntaxes into a canonical Universal AST, "
           "while "
           [:strong "Yin.vm"]
           " acts as the uncompromising, silicon-facing execution engine that runs flat instruction streams and continuations with absolute determinism. "
           "The Dao Engine maps this exact division of labor to real-time 3D simulation: "
           "authoring, scene representation, tooling, and polyglot scripting live on the high-level Yang side as passive datom streams, "
           "while physical simulation, state transitions, and rasterization live on the Yin.vm and "
           [:code "dao.postgraphics"]
           " side as flat, lock-free, zero-GC hardware execution."]
          [:p
           [:strong "The Dao Engine blueprint completes the circle:"]
           " "
           "it takes the relational simplicity and deterministic event streams of 1993 DOOM, "
           "and pairs them with the high-throughput, flat-buffer silicon execution of modern id Tech 7."]
          [:p
           "If we reconstruct interactive 3D simulation on datom.world primitives, the architecture decomposes into four clean, isolated layers linked only by streams:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-text"}
            "+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Producers: Editor (dao.scene / dao.gui) + Player Input Events    |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n                                |\n                       (Append-Only Stream)\n                                v\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| World Truth: Canonical Datom Stream [e a v t m]                  |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n        |                       |                       |\n (Index Observer)        (Delta Lowering)        (Event Observer)\n        v                       v                       v\n+-------------------+   +-------------------+   +------------------+\n| Query Engine      |   | Graphics VM       |   | Simulation VM    |\n| (dao.space.index) |   | (dao.postgraphics)|   | (yin.vm)         |\n| - Covered Indexes |   | - scene Lowering  |   | - Continuations  |\n| - Live Datalog    |   | - Flat Buffers    |   | - Pure Transition|\n| - Zero Loop Tax   |   | - 250 FPS Output  |   | - Emits Datoms   |\n+-------------------+   +-------------------+   +------------------+\n        |                                               |\n (Queries Feed UI)                               (Appends Batches)\n        v                                               v\n [Editor Viewport]                              [dao.stream Loop]"]]
          [:h3 "1. World State as Immutable Datoms"]
          [:p
           "In this re-envisioned model, an entity is merely an identity "
           [:code "e"]
           ". "
           "Its state consists of immutable facts over time:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-clojure"}
            ";; Entity 1042 is a physical object with a mesh, transform, and health\n[1042 :spatial/position [12.4 0.0 -4.2] 8021 nil]\n[1042 :render/mesh \"models/cyber_imp.mesh\" 8021 nil]\n[1042 :combat/health 100 8021 nil]"]]
          [:h3 "2. The Editor as an Independent Query Observer"]
          [:p
           "The editor does not live inside the game's execution memory. "
           "Instead, "
           [:code "dao.space.index"]
           " observes the datom stream and maintains queryable indexes in memory. "
           "When the level editor needs to populate an inspector panel, find all monsters in a room, or draw a scene hierarchy, it runs a pure Datalog query:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-clojure"}
            "[:find ?e ?pos ?health\n :where\n [?e :combat/health ?health]\n [?e :spatial/position ?pos]]"]]
          [:p
           "The editor has total introspection, full searchability, and instant live-editing capabilities. "
           "Yet the game loop does not know the editor exists, and pays zero CPU cycles for its reflection metadata."]
          [:h3
           "3. Rendering via scene.world and dao.postgraphics Lowering"]
          [:p
           "High-level 3D scene composition is expressed through "
           [:code "scene.world"]
           " (cameras, lights, meshes, materials) "
           "and lowered incrementally into a frame program of compact "
           [:code "dao.postgraphics"]
           " bytecode. "
           "The graphics VM is an isolated interpreter. It receives the bytecode stream and streams it straight into flat vertex buffers, bindless textures, and GPU draw calls. "
           "The renderer gets the pure, cache-friendly data density of DOOM and Bevy, while the scene author works with expressive declarative data."]
          [:h3
           "4. Gameplay Logic in yin.vm via Continuations and Polyglot Scripting"]
          [:p
           "Gameplay logic executes in "
           [:code "yin.vm"]
           " as pure transition functions:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-clojure"}
            ";; Pure transition: (State_t, Event) -> (State_{t+1}, StreamEffects)\n(defn on-projectile-hit [state event]\n  (let [target (:target event)\n        damage (:damage event)]\n    [(apply-damage state target damage)\n     [[:stream/emit :audio/play {:sound \"flesh_impact.wav\" :pos (:pos event)}]\n      [:stream/emit :particles/spawn {:effect \"blood_splatter\" :pos (:pos event)}]]]))"]]
          [:p
           "There are no callbacks and no hidden mutations. Systems emit events to streams, and other systems process them deterministically. "
           "Because computation in "
           [:code "yin.vm"]
           " is modeled as first-class, serializable continuations (Axiom 4), "
           "network prediction and rollback become effortless: "
           "if the server informs a client that a prediction was incorrect three frames ago, "
           "it simply resumes the continuation from the verified state snapshot and replays the stream events."]
          [:p
           "Furthermore, because of datom.world's "
           [:strong "Yang"]
           " compiler frontend, game scripting is not locked into a single language. "
           "Yang targets the Universal AST and compiles source text directly into executable datoms. "
           "As a result, Dao Engine can use any ANTLR-supported language to write gameplay scripts. "
           "Clojure and Python are supported out of the box today, but any grammar supported by ANTLR4 "
           "-- from TypeScript and Lua to custom domain-specific gameplay DSLs -- "
           "can compile down to the exact same Universal AST and run natively inside "
           [:code "yin.vm"]
           " with zero foreign-function boundary penalties."]
          [:p
           [:strong
            "This polyglot capability represents a human-centric design at the language level, rather than at the UI level."]
           " "
           "Engines like Unreal, Unity, and Godot pursued human-centric authoring by erecting massive, heavyweight UI layers on top of their engines: "
           "Blueprint graph editors, nested inspector panels, visual node graphs, and drag-and-drop authoring tools built for non-programmer content creators. "
           "Conversely, engines like DOOM and Bevy operate at the opposite extreme: pure, uncompromising code-only austerity that delivers blistering performance but demands low-level systems programming in C++ or Rust. "
           "The Dao Engine establishes a distinct middle tier between DOOM and those heavyweight visual tools: "
           "it empowers programmers and designers with the natural expressive power of whatever programming language they think in, "
           "without forcing them to build complex GUI bridges, manage fragile FFI boundaries, or surrender silicon-grade execution speed."]
          [:p
           "However, this does not mean visual authoring is abandoned. "
           [:strong
            "A high-level scene graph editor that incorporates polyglot language scripts can be built cleanly on top of Dao Engine without compromising runtime performance or system architecture."]
           " "
           "Unreal was forced to make devastating compromises to become user-friendly: "
           "it burdened every runtime object with virtual tables, heavy reflection descriptors (UHT), and garbage collection overhead just so the editor could inspect properties and serialize graphs. "
           "The Dao Engine has no need for such compromises. "
           "Because the visual editor and its scene graph live as independent stream observers ("
           [:code "dao.space.index"]
           " and "
           [:code "scene.world"]
           "), "
           "an artist can drag, drop, inspect, and wire polyglot scripts into entities in a rich visual editor, "
           "while the execution kernel ("
           [:code "yin.vm"]
           ") and renderer ("
           [:code "dao.postgraphics"]
           ") continue to churn through pure, lock-free, zero-GC contiguous silicon buffers. "
           "User-friendliness and uncompromised architectural purity are finally decoupled."]
          [:h2
           "The Historical Impasse: How Five Engines Hit Five Walls"]
          [:p
           "To understand why the Dao Engine architecture is necessary, one must understand how the industry arrived at its current impasse. "
           "In the history of real-time 3D simulation, game engine architecture has been defined by five distinct software philosophies:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "John Carmack's id Tech:"]
            " The Formula 1 racecar. Written in lean, procedural C and C++, it touched the hardware directly, achieving blistering frame rates with zero runtime garbage collection and flat memory buffers."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Tim Sweeney's Unreal Engine:"]
            " The operating system. A massive, monolithic C++ platform built on deep object-oriented inheritance, heavy reflection tables, and in-engine visual editing."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Joachim Ante's Unity:"]
            " The democratizer. A component-based engine that championed composition over inheritance, providing a friendly C# scripting layer over a native C++ runtime."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Juan Linietsky's Godot:"]
            " The indie tree-walker. A lightweight, nested scene-tree architecture driven by dynamically typed GDScript, signals, and pointer-linked node hierarchies."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Carter Anderson's Bevy:"]
            " The pure Rust ECS. A data-driven, archetype-based engine harnessing Rust's borrow checker for compile-time concurrency and cache-friendly linear memory."]]
          [:p
           "For decades, the industry has swung like a pendulum between these paradigms. "
           "If you want uncompromised, esports-grade 250 FPS performance, you write a custom data-oriented engine like DOOM or a Rust ECS like Bevy, but you sacrifice visual tooling and live iteration. "
           "If you want high-end cinematic graphics and visual scripting for massive studio teams, you endure Unreal's heavy C++ compile times, reflection bloat, and micro-stutter. "
           "If you want rapid prototyping and modular assembly, you accept Unity's garbage collection hitches and native-to-managed boundary taxes, or Godot's pointer-chasing node trees and dynamic signal sprawl."]
          [:p
           "All five paradigms have painted themselves into architectural dead ends. "
           "The sections that follow analyze why DOOM, Unreal, Unity, Godot, and Bevy each hit a fundamental wall, and why the solutions of the past three decades have only deepened the fracture."]
          [:h2
           "1. The Anatomy of DOOM: From 1993 Data Purity to Modern Titanium"]
          [:h3
           "The 1993 Epiphany: When Classic DOOM Anticipated Datoms"]
          [:p
           "Seen through the lens of datom.world, there is a striking historical insight: "
           [:strong
            "the original 1993 open-source DOOM engine was far closer to datom.world than almost any game engine built in the last twenty years"]
           ". "
           "In many respects, DOOM 1993 was already halfway to these principles: it understood that data should be passive syntax (WAD lumps), "
           "that entities are simple tuples (Things), and that simulation is a deterministic stream of events."]
          [:p
           "When John Carmack built the original DOOM in ANSI C, he did not think in terms of objects, hierarchies, or classes. "
           "He treated the engine not as an object-oriented simulation, but as an interpreter of passive relational data and deterministic event streams:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "WAD Files as Passive Syntax (Axiom 2):"]
            " A WAD file ('Where's All the Data?') was not an asset bundle of compiled classes. It was a flat directory of immutable, passive data lumps. Level geometry was stored as pure relational tables: VERTEXES, LINEDEFS, SIDEDEFS, SECTORS, and THINGS. The data held no behavior. The engine was simply an interpreter reading passive tables and projecting them into a view."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Entities as Tuples (Axiom 3):"]
            " In modern Unreal, an enemy is a two-kilobyte polymorphic class. In 1993 DOOM, a Thing in the WAD THINGS table (mapthing_t) was a discrete ten-byte 5-tuple of 16-bit integers: [x, y, angle, type, options]. Entity identity was simply its row index in the table. At runtime, entity behavior was driven by static state-machine tables (states and mobjinfo in info.c) rather than polymorphic class methods and dynamic inheritance."]
           [:li
            [:strong
             "Demos as Deterministic Event Streams (Axiom 1 and 4):"]
            " DOOM 1993 did not record demos as video files or position snapshots. A .lmp demo file was pure event-stream recording. The engine evaluated at a fixed 35 Hz tick rate (TICRATE = 35), recording a stream of 4-byte player inputs (ticcmd_t). Coupled with a fixed 256-byte pseudo-random table (P_Random in m_random.c), state transitions were pure deterministic functions: State_{t+1} = f(State_t, Event_t). Replaying a game was simply re-streaming input events through the pure transition kernel."]]
          [:h3 "The C++ Detour and Carmack's Functional Awakening"]
          [:p
           [:strong
            "The tragedy of the last thirty years is that game engines took a two-decade detour into C++ OOP, only to realize that hardware demanded data-oriented pipelines after all."]
           " "
           "In the early 2000s, C++ object-oriented mania swept the gaming industry. "
           "For DOOM 3 (2004 / id Tech 4), Carmack succumbed to the trend, rewriting the engine from scratch in C++. "
           "He introduced deep inheritance hierarchies (idClass -> idEntity -> idAnimatedEntity -> idActor -> idAI), polymorphic virtual tables (Think(), Damage()), "
           "and custom runtime reflection macros (idTypeInfo)."]
          [:p
           "Carmack quickly recognized the mistake. Encapsulating mutable state inside C++ classes introduced order-of-execution bugs, "
           "hidden side effects, and brittle dependencies. "
           "As chronicled across his "
           [:a
            {:href
             "https://github.com/ESWAT/john-carmack-plan-archive"}
            "historical .plan archives"]
           " and his internal memo "
           [:a
            {:href
             "http://number-none.com/blow/john_carmack_on_inlined_code.html"}
            "On Inlined Code"]
           ", Carmack had long distrusted deep abstraction layers that obscured execution flow. "
           "By 2012, Carmack articulated this evolution in his landmark essay "
           [:a
            {:href
             "https://www.gamedeveloper.com/programming/in-depth-functional-programming-in-c-"}
            "Functional Programming in C++"]
           ", arguing that mutable state is the root of software defects, "
           "and advocating for pure functions where the only inputs are explicit arguments and the only side effect is the return value."]
          [:h3
           "Modern id Tech 6 and 7: Radical DOD and the 250 FPS Racecar"]
          [:p
           "When id Software built DOOM (2016) on id Tech 6 and DOOM Eternal (2020) on id Tech 7 under Tiago Sousa and Billy Khan, "
           "they dismantled the legacy C++ OOP hierarchy and returned to Carmack's data-first philosophy, modernized for multicore processors:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Zero Heap Allocations During Gameplay:"]
            " When a level loads in DOOM, memory is allocated upfront in contiguous, fixed-size pools. During the heat of battle, the engine performs virtually zero dynamic memory allocation. There is no garbage collector, no reachability analysis, and no GC pauses."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Flat Contiguous Buffers:"]
            " Instead of an array of polymorphic Actor pointers, game state is stored in flat arrays of plain data structures. In id Tech 7, legacy MegaTextures were eliminated entirely in favor of fine-grained geometry and texture streaming over contiguous buffers. When the physics, animation, or AI systems run, they stream through memory linearly, maximizing CPU cache line utilization and SIMD vector execution."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Fiber-Based Cooperative Scheduling:"]
            " Introduced in id Tech 6 and perfected in id Tech 7, the engine breaks the work of each frame into hundreds of microsecond-scale jobs executed across user-space fibers. If a job is blocked waiting on an asset, the fiber yields instantly without putting the OS thread to sleep. The CPU logic frequently completes in under two milliseconds, leaving the remaining fourteen milliseconds of the frame purely for GPU rendering."]]
          [:p
           "Modern id Tech successfully reclaimed the hardware throughput of pure data. "
           "Yet in doing so, it lost two critical architectural strengths of 1993 DOOM: "
           "asynchronous GPU compute and non-deterministic multicore scheduling broke the pure input-stream replayability of 1993 demos, "
           "and passive WAD lumps were replaced by opaque, platform-locked binary asset blobs."]
          [:h3
           "The Engineering Tax: Why Non-Programmers Cannot Build a DOOM Game"]
          [:p
           "The speed of DOOM comes with a severe human cost: "
           [:strong
            "building a game on id Tech strictly requires specialized software engineers at every turn"]
           "."]
          [:p
           "In Unreal Engine, an artist or game designer can build and ship an entire game using Blueprints without writing a single line of C++. "
           "In DOOM, that is completely impossible. id Tech has no visual scripting VM. "
           "Gameplay mechanics, demon AI behaviors (such as the Marauder's shield parry), and player abilities (such as the Meat Hook) "
           "must be written in raw, modern C++ by gameplay engineers."]
          [:p
           "Furthermore, the zero-allocation rule creates a massive systems engineering burden:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Manual Memory Budgeting:"]
            " A level designer cannot simply drop fifty extra demons or a new particle emitter into an encounter. All memory pools are pre-dimensioned at map load. Systems engineers must manually calculate cache-line-aligned struct layouts and guarantee that fixed buffers will never overflow during combat."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Fiber Concurrency Discipline:"]
            " Writing micro-jobs that yield across sixteen CPU cores without lock contention requires deep systems mastery of atomic memory fences, cache-line bouncing, and lock-free ring buffers. Designers cannot touch this code."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Low-Level Shaders:"]
            " To hit a six-millisecond frame budget, graphics engineers must write bespoke Vulkan and DirectX 12 compute kernels and bindless resource tables rather than relying on automatic material graphs."]]
          [:p
           "Level designers in id's internal editor ("
           [:code "idStudio"]
           ") can block out geometry and tweak exposed numeric parameters. "
           "But the moment a designer wants a novel mechanic, a custom camera behavior, or a new weapon interaction, "
           "a C++ software engineer must architect and compile it from scratch."]
          [:p
           "This brings us to "
           [:strong "The Wall of Silicon Rigidity"]
           ". "
           "DOOM's data layouts are so tightly coupled to the silicon and frozen at map load that they offer no natural hook for dynamic inspection or live, visual iteration. "
           "Because the engine has no runtime reflection layer and no dynamic scripting VM, "
           "content creation demands an elite, expensive team of systems engineers, technical artists, and custom offline baking pipelines."]
          [:h2 "2. The Anatomy of Unreal: The Monolithic OOP Facade"]
          [:p
           "Unreal Engine won the commercial AAA market because it catered to content creators: artists, level designers, and technical animators. "
           "To make life intuitive for humans, Unreal adopted the classical Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) paradigm."]
          [:p
           "In Unreal, everything in the world is an "
           [:code "AActor"]
           ". "
           "An Actor is a massive C++ object packed with virtual function pointer tables, component arrays, and reflection metadata. "
           "Actors inherit through deep, rigid taxonomies:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-text"}
            "UObject -> AActor -> APawn -> ACharacter -> APlayerCharacter"]]
          [:p
           "To allow the editor to inspect properties, serialize levels, and expose variables to Blueprints visual scripting, "
           "Epic created the Unreal Header Tool (UHT). Every property annotated with "
           [:code "UPROPERTY()"]
           " "
           "is tracked by generated reflection metadata. While this makes building an editor UI relatively straightforward, "
           "it exacts a devastating architectural toll at runtime:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Cache Destruction:"]
            " Game entities live scattered across the heap behind pointers. Iterating over actors means chasing pointers through memory, incurring constant L1 and L2 cache misses."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Hidden Re-entrancy and Tick Hell:"]
            " Because entities encapsulate mutable state, calling a method like "
            [:code "TakeDamage()"]
            " triggers cascades of delegates and side effects. If Actor A mutates Actor B during an iteration loop, iterator invalidation and order-of-execution bugs follow. Unreal had to invent complex TickGroup staging and prerequisite arrays just to keep its game loop from collapsing."]
           [:li
            [:strong "The State Synchronization Tax:"]
            " Over half of all gameplay bugs stem from state desynchronization. A character died on the server, but the client animation blueprint is still in the Run state, while the UI health bar shows five percent. Keeping mutable C++ object graphs in sync across network boundaries requires thousands of lines of fragile boilerplate."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Micro-Stutter and Frame Pacing:"]
            " Periodic garbage collection sweeps, runtime memory allocation, and heavy Game Thread actor ticks cause random frame-time spikes that break the visual illusion of real-time simulation."]]
          [:p
           "The supreme irony is that Tim Sweeney himself has long recognized this flaw. "
           "In his famous 2006 POPL keynote, "
           [:a
            {:href
             "https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~dpw/popl/06/Tim-POPL.pdf"}
            "The Next Mainstream Programming Language: A Game Developer's Perspective"]
           ", Sweeney argued that shared mutable state is the primary obstacle to concurrent software, "
           "praising functional programming and immutability. "
           "Yet the commercial reality of Unreal Engine's legacy codebase kept it chained to mutable OOP for twenty more years."]
          [:h2 "3. The Anatomy of Unity: The Managed Component Trap"]
          [:p
           "Unity entered the industry with a revolutionary insight: "
           [:strong "composition over inheritance"]
           ". "
           "Instead of Unreal's deep "
           [:code "AActor"]
           " class hierarchies, Unity introduced the "
           [:code "GameObject"]
           " as a lightweight container, "
           "allowing developers to attach reusable "
           [:code "MonoBehaviour"]
           " components. "
           "Combined with C# scripting, Unity democratized game development for millions of creators."]
          [:p
           "Yet Unity's architecture introduced its own catastrophic trade-offs:"]
          [:h3 "The Native-Managed Chasm: InternalCalls and P/Invoke"]
          [:p
           "Unity is an engine split in half. The low-level engine (rendering, physics, transform hierarchy) is written in native C++. "
           "The gameplay layer is written in managed C# running on Mono or IL2CPP. "
           "Every time a script queries a property like "
           [:code "transform.position"]
           ", execution must cross the "
           [:strong
            "native-to-managed marshaling boundary (via InternalCalls or P/Invoke)"]
           ". "
           "The runtime must dereference and validate the managed wrapper's underlying native C++ pointer, copy vector values across runtimes, "
           "and enforce engine-side thread checks. "
           "A loop querying the positions of a thousand entities spends more time crossing the language border than doing real math."]
          [:h3 "The Boehm GC Nightmare"]
          [:p
           "Because C# allocates reference objects on the heap, everyday gameplay operations (spawning projectiles, string formatting, LINQ queries, delegates) "
           "generate managed garbage. "
           "Unity's Boehm-Demers-Weiser garbage collector is non-compacting and historically stop-the-world. "
           "Every few seconds, the engine halts the main thread to sweep memory, creating the infamous 'Unity frame drop' that plagued mobile and console games for a generation."]
          [:h3 "The Tragedy of Unity DOTS: The Schism of Two Worlds"]
          [:p
           "In 2018, Unity recognized that its "
           [:code "GameObject"]
           " architecture had hit an absolute ceiling. "
           "In response, Unity launched an ambitious, multi-year project to rebuild the engine around "
           [:strong "DOTS (Data-Oriented Technology Stack)"]
           ", combining a pure C# ECS with the Burst compiler and Job System."]
          [:p
           "The result was an architectural quagmire that fractured the Unity community for seven years. "
           "Why did DOTS struggle so painfully? "
           "Because Unity attempted to maintain "
           [:strong
            "two completely incompatible world models simultaneously"]
           ":"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            "The user-friendly "
            [:strong "GameObject"]
            " world (OOP, garbage-collected, inspector-friendly)."]
           [:li
            "The high-performance "
            [:strong "Entities"]
            " world (DOD, unmanaged structs, cache-friendly, zero classes)."]]
          [:p
           "To bridge this chasm, Unity had to invent "
           [:strong "Authoring Components"]
           ", "
           [:strong "Bakers"]
           ", and source-generated translation pipelines. "
           "Developers were forced to author their game twice: once as an authoring component for the inspector, "
           "and once as an unmanaged struct for the runtime. "
           "The abstraction was leaky, brittle, and exhausting. Unity proved that "
           [:strong
            "you cannot bridge an OOP authoring model to a DOD runtime by adding code generation and baking glue"]
           "."]
          [:h2 "4. The Anatomy of Godot: The Pure Scene Tree Walker"]
          [:p
           "Godot took the indie development world by storm by offering a clean, lightweight alternative to commercial giants. "
           "Juan Linietsky designed Godot around an intuitive mental model: everything is a "
           [:code "Node"]
           ", "
           "and every game is a tree of nodes (the "
           [:code "SceneTree"]
           "). "
           "Scenes are nested modularly, instanced dynamically, and wired together using a lightweight scripting language, GDScript."]
          [:h3 "The Node Tree and the Dynamic Signal Web"]
          [:p
           "Under the hood, Godot's scene tree is a classical object-oriented tree of C++ pointers. "
           "Every frame, the engine recursively walks the tree, dispatching virtual lifecycle methods like "
           [:code "_process(delta)"]
           " and "
           [:code "_physics_process(delta)"]
           " across heap-allocated objects."]
          [:p
           "To decouple nodes, Godot relies heavily on "
           [:strong "Signals"]
           " (the observer pattern). "
           "An area node emits a signal like "
           [:code "body_entered"]
           ", and any other node can connect to it dynamically with a callable callback. "
           "While this makes prototyping accessible to beginners, it creates a labyrinth of implicit control flow:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Dynamic Signal Cascades:"]
            " Emitting a signal triggers immediate, synchronous callbacks across arbitrary nodes. If node A's signal mutates node B, which emits a signal mutating node C, execution order becomes non-deterministic and re-entrant. This violates the foundational datom.world invariant: no implicit callbacks, every event must flow through an explicit append-only stream."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Lack of Global World Truth:"]
            " Game state does not exist in an identifiable, queryable database. State is fragmented across private variables of isolated nodes. Finding all entities with specific gameplay properties requires walking the tree with fragile path lookups like "
            [:code "get_node(\"../Player/Weapon\")"]
            " or string-based group searches."]]
          [:h3 "The Dual-Interpreted Performance Wall"]
          [:p
           "Godot compounds its cache-hostile tree structure with language overhead. "
           "GDScript is an interpreted, dynamically typed bytecode language. When a script updates an entity's transform:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-gdscript"}
            "position += velocity * delta"]]
          [:p
           "The GDScript virtual machine must decode bytecode opcodes, dispatch through 24-byte dynamically typed "
           [:code "Variant"]
           " tagged unions, "
           "and validate C++ pointers through "
           [:code "ObjectDB"]
           " lookups. Godot pays a double execution tax: "
           "pointer-chasing across a heap of C++ nodes, compounded by dynamic dispatch and type-tag branch checks for every property access."]
          [:p
           "While developers can write performance-critical logic in C# or C++ via GDExtension, "
           "the underlying data structure remains an in-memory tree of heap-allocated pointers. "
           "When an indie developer attempts to scale Godot from a simple platformer to a dense bullet-hell, a massive RTS, "
           "or a procedural voxel simulation with tens of thousands of active entities, frame rates collapse. "
           "The CPU spends the vast majority of its cycles stalled on cache misses while walking node pointers."]
          [:p
           "Godot's official workaround for high entity counts is telling developers to bypass the "
           [:code "SceneTree"]
           " entirely "
           "and issue imperative Resource ID (RID) commands directly to low-level servers ("
           [:code "RenderingServer"]
           " and "
           [:code "PhysicsServer3D"]
           "). "
           "This proves the fundamental thesis: the moment you need real hardware performance in Godot, "
           "you are forced to abandon its authoring model completely."]
          [:h2
           "5. The Anatomy of Bevy: The Rust ECS and the Tooling Desert"]
          [:p
           "At the opposite end of the modern spectrum sits "
           [:strong "Bevy"]
           ", created by Carter Anderson. "
           "Bevy threw away legacy OOP and built an engine from first principles around a pure Entity Component System (ECS) in Rust, "
           "designed to extract every drop of multicore performance from modern hardware."]
          [:h3 "The Archetype ECS and Compile-Time Concurrency"]
          [:p
           "In Bevy, an entity is merely a 64-bit integer ID ("
           [:code "Entity"]
           "). "
           "Components are plain Rust data structs stored contiguously in memory tables by "
           [:strong "archetype"]
           " "
           "(the unique set of component types an entity possesses). When a system processes entities:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-rust"}
            "fn movement_system(mut query: Query<(&mut Transform, &Velocity)>) {\n    for (mut transform, velocity) in &mut query {\n        transform.translation += velocity.linvel;\n    }\n}"]]
          [:p
           "The loop streams through flat, contiguous arrays of data. "
           "L1 and L2 cache lines are saturated with near-perfect efficiency, and SIMD vectorization operates effortlessly. "
           "Even more impressively, Bevy uses Rust's static type system and borrow checker to schedule systems: "
           "because the scheduler knows at compile time which systems access which components mutably or immutably, "
           "it executes non-conflicting systems concurrently across thread pools with zero locks."]
          [:h3 "The Rigidity of Static Types and the Missing Editor"]
          [:p
           "Yet Bevy runs headfirst into a different architectural wall: "
           [:strong "The Tooling Desert"]
           " and "
           [:strong "Static Type Rigidity"]
           "."]
          [:p
           "Because Bevy's architecture is anchored in Rust's compile-time type system, modifying entity composition at runtime is expensive:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Archetype Migration Overhead:"]
            " Adding or removing a component dynamically at runtime forces Bevy to move the entity from one archetype table to another, physically copying all its remaining components between memory buffers. While Bevy introduced SparseSet storage as an opt-in alternative to avoid table copies, doing so forces developers to commit to rigid storage layouts at compile time and forfeits contiguous SIMD table iteration. In dynamic, interactive worlds where game state changes fluidly, archetype churning remains an inherent architectural friction."]
           [:li
            [:strong "The Missing Live Editor:"]
            " Rust has no native runtime reflection, no dynamic scripting VM, and no REPL. While Bevy provides "
            [:code "bevy_reflect"]
            ", building an interactive visual editor has been a grueling, multi-year challenge. Game designers and technical artists cannot drag-and-drop entities, tweak logic on the fly, or inspect arbitrary world state without re-entering the Rust compiler."]
           [:li
            [:strong "The Compilation Barrier:"]
            " Rust's heavy generic monomorphization and macro expansion impose brutal compilation cycles. Even with fast incremental linkers like Mold, tweaking gameplay logic requires waiting seconds or minutes for a rebuild, destroying the rapid creative flow required for game design."]]
          [:p
           "Bevy achieved Carmack-level data density and thread safety, but locked its world into compiled binary machine code. "
           "Like DOOM, it remains an engine built by systems engineers for systems engineers, stranding creators in a code-only desert."]
          [:h2 "The Root Fallacy Across All Five Engines"]
          [:p
           "Why did all five engines paint themselves into a corner? "
           "Because they all committed the same foundational error: "
           [:strong
            "they assumed that the authoring model, the state store, and the execution engine must share the same memory representation"]
           "."]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Unreal:"]
            " Forced the execution engine to run on the authoring model (fat polymorphic C++ objects). Great tooling, sluggish execution."]
           [:li
            [:strong "DOOM:"]
            " Forced the authoring model to conform to the execution engine (flat rigid arrays). Blistering execution, rigid tooling."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Unity:"]
            " Split itself in half with C++ and C#, and later tried to glue two incompatible models together with DOTS bakers. Leaky abstractions and massive friction."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Godot:"]
            " Nested both authoring and execution into an in-memory scene tree of pointers, creating dynamic signal chaos and cache starvation at scale."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Bevy:"]
            " Welded the execution model to Rust's compile-time type system. Blistering throughput, but stranded in a code-only tooling desert."]]
          [:h2
           "The Proven Precedent: How We Solved the AST Walker vs. Bytecode Paradox"]
          [:p
           "This conflict is not unique to 3D game engines. "
           "In fact, the exact same battle has played out for decades in compiler and virtual machine design. "
           "In datom.world, we faced this exact dilemma when building "
           [:code "yin.vm"]
           "."]
          [:p
           "As we documented in "
           [:a
            {:href "/blog/ast-datom-streams-bytecode-performance.blog"}
            "AST Datom Streams: Bytecode Performance with Semantic Preservation"]
           ", traditional wisdom dictated an impossible choice:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "The AST Tree-Walker:"]
            " Traverses nested maps and node objects recursively across the heap. It preserves full semantic meaning, original names, source locations, and queryability. But it is slow: pointer chasing, cache misses, and recursive function call overhead cripple performance."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Traditional Numeric Bytecode:"]
            " Flattens the program into a contiguous array of raw bytes or opcodes. It delivers high-speed linear memory access and instruction prefetching. But it erases all semantics: high-level intent, variable names, and queryability are completely lost."]]
          [:p
           "The breakthrough came when we realized the assumption was wrong: "
           [:strong
            "bytecode does not have to erase semantics, and an AST does not have to be an in-memory tree"]
           "."]
          [:p
           "In datom.world, we split the program into two complementary representations over append-only streams:"]
          [:ol
           [:li
            [:strong "The AST Structure Stream:"]
            " Canonical, immutable datoms representing the program's semantic facts [node-id :ast/type :application tx nil]. Queryable, introspectable, and never lost (unitarity)."]
           [:li
            [:strong "The Linear Execution Stream:"]
            " An ordered sequence of execution steps [step-id :exec/instruction :eval-operand tx nil] and [step-id :exec/order 1 tx nil]."]]
          [:p
           "Using a pure Datalog compilation query, we flattened the recursive AST tree into a linear execution stream. "
           "Instead of recursive pointer-chasing traversal ("
           [:code "eval-ast"]
           "), execution became a tight, cache-friendly linear loop:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-clojure"}
            ";; Sequential iteration over the flattened execution stream\n(defn run-execution-stream [db exec-stream]\n  (reduce\n   (fn [state step]\n     (let [instruction (:exec/instruction step)\n           node (:exec/node step)]\n       (execute-step state instruction node)))\n   initial-state\n   (sort-by :exec/order exec-stream)))"]]
          [:p
           "The real-world validation of this architecture arrived in our benchmarks, recorded in "
           [:a
            {:href "/blog/a-tale-of-four-vm-benchmarks.blog"}
            "A tale of 4 Virtual Machines: Universal AST Benchmarks"]
           ". By projecting the datom stream into a flat, array-backed index with primitive integer dispatch and an Object array stack, "
           "the Semantic VM slashed execution time from approximately 27 milliseconds down to 4.38 milliseconds. "
           "It moved from being the slowest engine to outperforming the native Clojure AST Walker by fifty percent, "
           "all while maintaining complete semantic linkage back to the source datoms."]
          [:h2
           "The Isomorphism: Game Engines are Just Virtual Machines"]
          [:p
           "When you see this connection, the architectural clashes across Unreal, DOOM, Unity, Godot, and Bevy cease to be a mystery. "
           [:strong
            "A game engine is topologically identical to a virtual machine:"]]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Unreal's Actor Hierarchy is an AST Tree-Walker:"]
            " The Game Thread traverses a deep tree of polymorphic C++ heap pointers. It suffers from the exact same random memory access, pointer chasing, and cache misses as a recursive AST evaluator."]
           [:li
            [:strong "DOOM's Engine is a Bytecode VM:"]
            " It throws away high-level entity abstractions, leaving behind only flat packed memory buffers for the GPU and fiber scheduler. It achieves blistering execution, but at the cost of opacity and authoring rigidity."]
           [:li
            [:strong
             "Unity is a Bridge Between Incompatible Runtimes:"]
            " It attempts to run a tree-walker on top of a bytecode substrate, incurring P/Invoke marshaling costs and forcing developers to write awkward bakers to translate between them."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Godot is a Dynamic Bytecode Tree-Walker:"]
            " It runs a dynamically typed bytecode interpreter (GDScript) traversing a pointer-linked C++ scene graph, drowning in cache misses, dynamic boxing, and signal re-entrancy."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Bevy is a Statically Compiled Bytecode VM:"]
            " It compiles archetype queries directly into native machine instructions with zero runtime reflection, achieving ultimate throughput at the total expense of runtime introspection and dynamic live authoring."]]
          [:p
           "The solution that solved the virtual machine paradox is the exact solution that builds the holy grail game engine: "
           [:strong
            "maintain the canonical world as an immutable datom stream, and project it into flat linear execution streams for the hardware"]
           "."]
          [:h2
           "The Datom.World Paradigm: One Stream, Many Interpretations"]
          [:p
           "The core architecture of "
           [:strong "datom.world"]
           " formalizes this through its foundational axioms:"]
          [:ol
           [:li
            [:strong "Everything is a Stream:"]
            " All IO and data flow through append-only streams."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Interpretation Creates Semantics:"]
            " Data is passive syntax. Semantics only emerge through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Code and State are Datoms:"]
            " Runtime state and executable code are structured datoms [e a v t m], enabling syntax independence and structural reflection."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Everything is a Continuation:"]
            " Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere."]]
          [:p
           "Crucially, datom.world enforces non-negotiable invariants: "
           "no hidden global state, no implicit control flow, no callbacks (every callback is an event on a stream), "
           [:strong "do not assume graphs"]
           " (graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples, not baked in as pointer graphs), "
           [:strong "do not lose information"]
           " (unitarity: streams are append-only; the Universal AST and canonical world datoms are never destroyed), and "
           [:strong
            "never collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer"]
           "."]
          [:h2 "Dismantling the Three Universal Walls"]
          [:p
           "When you project this substrate into real-time simulation, the architectural trade-offs that fractured Unreal, DOOM, Unity, Godot, and Bevy dissolve. "
           "All five engines hit one of three universal walls:"]
          [:h3
           "Wall 1: Live Authoring vs. Packed Silicon (Resolving DOOM and Bevy)"]
          [:p
           "How does a designer get instant visual feedback without ruining DOOM's packed memory buffers or waiting on Bevy's Rust compiler?"]
          [:p
           "Just as we compiled AST datoms into a linear VM execution stream, "
           "when an artist drags a light onto an entity or tweaks a slider in the viewport, "
           "the editor appends an immutable datom to the scene stream:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-clojure"}
            "[entity-id :light/intensity 4.5 tx-id nil]"]]
          [:p
           "The graphics subsystem ("
           [:code "dao.postgraphics"]
           ") acts as an incremental lowering observer. "
           "Because lowering is a pure function over stream deltas, it recompiles the modified scene fragment into GPU uniform buffers in under twenty milliseconds. "
           "The artist experiences instant, lag-free visual manipulation; the GPU continues to execute against pure, flat contiguous buffers. "
           "Live mutation and packed silicon layout are completely reconciled."]
          [:h3
           "Wall 2: Expressive Composition vs. Runtime Throughput (Resolving Unity and Godot)"]
          [:p
           "Unity tried to bridge its divide with complex DOTS bakers and source generators; "
           "Godot forced developers to choose between cache-hostile node trees or bypassing the scene tree with raw imperative Server commands. "
           "Datom.world avoids both traps because "
           [:strong "Code and State are already unified Datoms"]
           " (Axiom 3)."]
          [:p
           "In this re-envisioned model, an entity is not a C++ class, a C# object, a Godot node, or a rigid Rust ECS struct. "
           "An entity is merely an identity. Any attribute assigned to an entity is automatically an indexed fact:"]
          [:pre
           [:code
            {:class "language-clojure"}
            ";; Datalog query used by the editor inspector to discover all entity attributes\n[:find ?attr ?val\n :where\n [1042 ?attr ?val]]"]]
          [:p
           "There is no impedance mismatch between an authoring layer and an execution layer. "
           "There is no P/Invoke boundary because data crosses stream boundaries as passive syntax. "
           "There are no baking scripts because the Datalog query engine ("
           [:code "dao.space"]
           ") indexes the stream directly for the editor. "
           "Meanwhile, simulation logic in "
           [:code "yin.vm"]
           " executes as a flat, linear continuation loop. "
           "You gain the effortless malleability of visual scripting without writing a single line of authoring-to-runtime boilerplate, "
           "and without requiring an army of C++ systems engineers to hand-craft every memory buffer. "
           "The lowering compiler handles silicon layout automatically."]
          [:h3
           "Wall 3: The Malleability Flywheel (Resolving Unreal's Billion-Dollar Tooling Tax)"]
          [:p
           "Epic Games and Unity spent hundreds of millions of dollars building imperative editors on top of mutable memory. "
           "They had to manually write undo stacks, asset serializers, GC workarounds, and cross-language thread synchronizers."]
          [:p
           "Under this architecture, the features that make commercial editors expensive are native mathematical consequences of the substrate:"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Undo and Redo:"]
            " Free. Because datoms carry transaction time "
            [:code "t"]
            ", undoing an edit is simply querying the state of the world as of transaction "
            [:code "t - 1"]
            ". There are no undo stacks to maintain."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Save and Load:"]
            " Free. The world state is an append-only stream of facts. Saving is recording a stream offset; loading is streaming the facts into an observer."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Declarative UI:"]
            " Tooling interfaces are authored in "
            [:code "dao.scene"]
            " (for retained editor UI) or "
            [:code "dao.gui"]
            " (for immediate UI) and compiled into "
            [:code "dao.postgraphics"]
            " bytecode. There is no heavy, brittle C++ or C# UI framework required."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Zero-Allocation Concurrency:"]
            " Deterministic stream batching eliminates stop-the-world garbage collection pauses forever."]
           [:li
            [:strong "Multiplayer Replication:"]
            " Free. Client and server are simply two stream observers synchronizing append-only datom logs."]]
          [:h2 "Comparison Matrix: The Six Paradigms"]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li
            [:strong "Entity Architecture:"]
            [:ul.bulleted
             [:li
              "Unreal Engine 5: Heavy polymorphic C++ classes (AActor) with virtual tables, pointers, and GC headers."]
             [:li
              "id Tech 7 (DOOM): Rigid flat arrays and packed structs, manually tuned per game."]
             [:li
              "Unity: Hybrid GameObjects + MonoBehaviours split across C++ and C#; awkward DOTS ECS struct authoring."]
             [:li
              "Godot: Tree of C++ Node objects with dynamic signal connections and Variant dictionaries."]
             [:li
              "Bevy: Contiguous archetype tables indexed by 64-bit integer IDs (Entity), statically typed in Rust."]
             [:li
              "The Dao Engine (Blueprint): Entity identities with immutable facts [e a v t m]. Zero memory overhead on execution, infinite malleability in authoring."]]]
           [:li
            [:strong "Execution Topology:"]
            [:ul.bulleted
             [:li
              "Unreal Engine 5: Recursive AST-walker style traversal of heap-allocated actor components."]
             [:li
              "id Tech 7 (DOOM): Flat contiguous arrays and micro-job loops."]
             [:li
              "Unity: Virtual method invocations crossing the native/managed P/Invoke boundary on every Update()."]
             [:li
              "Godot: Recursive scene-tree traversal with GDScript bytecode dispatch and dynamic signal callbacks."]
             [:li
              "Bevy: Concurrent system dispatch over contiguous archetype slices, scheduled via compile-time type analysis."]
             [:li
              "The Dao Engine (Blueprint): Canonical AST/state datoms compiled to a linear execution stream with flat array backing and integer dispatch."]]]
           [:li
            [:strong "Tooling, Scripting, and Reflection:"]
            [:ul.bulleted
             [:li
              "Unreal Engine 5: C++ and Blueprints; runtime reflection tables (UHT) baked into every object with high memory and CPU cost."]
             [:li
              "id Tech 7 (DOOM): Pure C++; minimal runtime reflection; requires specialized offline baking pipelines."]
             [:li
              "Unity: C# reflection for MonoBehaviours; complex external bakers and code generators for DOTS entities."]
             [:li
              "Godot: GDScript and C#; lightweight runtime ClassDB reflection, but tightly coupled to the C++ node hierarchy."]
             [:li
              "Bevy: Rust only; compile-time type reflection (bevy_reflect); no runtime REPL, no dynamic scripting, and no mature visual editor."]
             [:li
              "The Dao Engine (Blueprint): Polyglot via Yang (Clojure and Python supported today, any ANTLR4 language pluggable); Datalog query engine (dao.space) observes the stream independently with zero runtime tax on the execution kernel."]]]
           [:li
            [:strong
             "Engineering Barrier and Developer Accessibility:"]
            [:ul.bulleted
             [:li
              "Unreal Engine 5: Highly accessible to non-programmers; artists and designers can build and ship full games with Blueprints."]
             [:li
              "id Tech 7 (DOOM): Extremely high engineering barrier; completely impossible without specialized C++ systems engineers for all mechanics, memory pools, and fiber jobs."]
             [:li
              "Unity: Accessible via C# and visual scripting, but fractured when trying to author high-performance DOTS ECS code."]
             [:li
              "Godot: Extremely accessible and beginner-friendly with GDScript and node scenes, but hits a hard wall when scaling to high-performance simulation."]
             [:li
              "Bevy: High engineering barrier; requires deep Rust expertise, wrestling with the borrow checker and archetype migration rules."]
             [:li
              "The Dao Engine (Blueprint): Democratized; designers author high-level declarative facts while the lowering compiler automatically produces flat, lock-free silicon execution."]]]
           [:li
            [:strong "Memory and Concurrency:"]
            [:ul.bulleted
             [:li
              "Unreal Engine 5: Coarse thread pipelining with 1-frame latency queues, prone to GC hitches and tick ordering conflicts."]
             [:li
              "id Tech 7 (DOOM): Lock-free fiber job scheduler; flat 144 to 250 FPS frame pacing."]
             [:li
              "Unity: Stop-the-world Boehm GC pauses and script execution order race conditions."]
             [:li
              "Godot: Single-threaded main scene loop; physics running on separate thread with mutex locks; prone to cache misses."]
             [:li
              "Bevy: Safe multi-threaded system execution via Rust borrow checker; contiguous archetype memory layout."]
             [:li
              "The Dao Engine (Blueprint): Pure stream processing with serializable continuations; deterministic, lock-free, and hitch-free."]]]
           [:li
            [:strong "Time, Replay, and Multiplayer:"]
            [:ul.bulleted
             [:li
              "Unreal Engine 5: Custom replication macros, bespoke save serialization, manual rollback buffers."]
             [:li
              "id Tech 7 (DOOM): Custom netcode packet serializers, rigid level state snapshots."]
             [:li
              "Unity: Fragmented netcode packages; difficult rollback due to mutable heap allocations."]
             [:li
              "Godot: High-level multiplayer node replication, but manual state serialization and no native time-travel rollback."]
             [:li
              "Bevy: Rollback possible via manual component snapshots, but archetype restructuring makes deep state serialization complex."]
             [:li
              "The Dao Engine (Blueprint): Native time dimension in every datom; rewind, replay, save/load, and continuation migration come built-in."]]]]
          [:h2 "The Conclusion: Moving Beyond the 1990s Compromise"]
          [:p
           "For three decades, game architecture has been held hostage by false compromises: "
           "that performance requires sacrificing developer ergonomics, and that developer ergonomics requires sacrificing hardware performance."]
          [:p
           "Unreal proved that developers will tolerate massive architectural complexity and sluggish runtimes if the tooling allows them to build worlds quickly. "
           "DOOM proved that modern hardware is capable of astounding, buttery-smooth cinematic simulation when freed from object-oriented baggage. "
           "Unity proved that developers crave composition and rapid scripting, but suffered because it could not reconcile an OOP authoring model with a data-oriented hardware reality. "
           "Godot proved the joy of a lightweight, modular scene tree, but demonstrated the hard ceiling of pointer-chasing nodes and dynamic signal webs. "
           "Bevy proved that pure ECS in Rust can achieve unmatched concurrency and cache locality, but underscored the painful creative tax of locking state into a static type system without live visual authoring."]
          [:p
           "Datom.world demonstrates that this conflict was born of a category error: "
           "the conflation of syntax with semantics, and the collapse of interpretation into execution. "
           "Just as we proved in Yin.vm that an AST does not need to be tree-walked to remain queryable, "
           "a game entity does not need to be a heavy C++ or C# object to be inspectable and editable."]
          [:p
           "By compiling immutable datom streams into linear execution streams, the Dao Engine blueprint shows how to give the artist the power of rich, declarative queryability, "
           "while giving the silicon the pure, uninterrupted stream of instructions it was designed to devour. "
           "It completes the circle: reconciling the relational simplicity and deterministic event streams of 1993 DOOM "
           "with the blistering, lock-free silicon throughput of modern id Tech 7."]
          [:p
           "The holy grail game engine will not be built by adding more macros to C++, bolting ECS hacks onto legacy OOP frameworks, or writing translation bakers between two alien runtimes. "
           "It will be built on the foundation that everything is data, everything is a stream, and interpretation creates semantics."]]]]}
